    "available_at": "2026-10-28T08:06:57.249738+00:00",
    "body": "Körber is acquiring a stake in NAiSE, a German software company whose fleet management platform coordinates warehouse robots and manually operated industrial vehicles from different manufacturers . Announced Oct. 22, the investment expands the companies' existing partnership .\nWhat’s Related\nThe companies plan to connect NAiSE’s platform more closely with Körber’s warehouse technology and develop additional uses for the software. Körber did not disclose the investment amount or the size of its stake.\nFounded in 2017, NAiSE had financed its business independently before the investment. Its software brings automated guided vehicles, autonomous mobile robots and manually operated industrial trucks into a shared system to coordinate material movement in warehouses and factories.\n“We deliberately chose a partner that respects our independence and also helps us scale internationally,” said Kai Przybysz-Herz, co-founder and CEO of NAiSE. The company will continue working with other system integrators and vehicle manufacturers.\nConnecting vehicles from different systems\nThe software addresses a practical challenge for warehouses using equipment from several manufacturers: coordinating vehicles that otherwise work through separate systems. NAiSE says its platform provides a common layer for managing those fleets.\nAccording to NAiSE, its Fleet Manager brings mobile robots, forklifts , tugger trains, and other manually operated vehicles into one system. A central control layer coordinates tasks across the different vehicles, giving managers a shared view of the fleet.\nThe platform also links fleet management to transport orders, traffic coordination and warehouse management . Its Order Manager translates requests from business and warehouse systems into transport tasks and assigns work across manually operated vehicles and autonomous robots.\nNAiSE says its Traffic Manager coordinates vehicle movement and infrastructure through shared control logic. The software provides real-time visibility , routing and simulation tools to plan how mixed fleets move through a facility.\nWorking with existing warehouse software\nKörber says NAiSE’s integration tools connect vehicles and systems regardless of which warehouse management, execution, or control software a customer uses. Those systems manage inventory , organize work, and direct equipment inside a facility.\nThe companies intend to deepen the connection between NAiSE and Körber’s warehouse products while continuing to develop the platform for production logistics . Existing robot-manufacturer partnerships will also remain available to joint and new customers.\n“Körber brings deep industrial experience, a global customer network and a genuine understanding of entrepreneurial thinking,” added Przybysz-Herz.",
